{"ID": "soap_00097", "dialogue": "What's going on with you? What brings you here today? I am having loose watery stools for more than two weeks now. I feel very lethargic. I also have pain in my tummy.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Loose watery stools\n- Severity: Not explicitly mentioned\n- Duration: More than two weeks\n- Associated symptoms: Lethargy, pain in tummy\n- Relevant medical history: Not provided\n- Family history: Not provided\n- Allergies: Not provided\n- Other concerns: None explicitly mentioned\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00054", "dialogue": "Hi there! How are doing today? I am doing well. How have you been doing with keeping your hemoglobin levels between four and five point six prercent? I have been doing well. I have been able to keep it within that range.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient reports they have been doing well with keeping their hemoglobin levels between 4% and 5.6%.\n- No additional symptoms, severity, duration, associated symptoms, relevant medical history, family history, allergies, or concerns explicitly mentioned.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation, diagnoses, or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00074", "dialogue": "Have you had a recent vaccination? I had a flu shot last winter but I have not had and recent vaccines. Do you have any planned in the near future? I was going to get another flu shot but I don't think I am due for that for another three months. Great.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient had a flu shot last winter.\n- Patient has not had any recent vaccines.\n- Patient plans to get another flu shot in the near future but thinks they are not due for it for another three months.\n\nObjective:\n- (No objective data provided.)\n\nAssessment:\n- (No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.)\n\nPlan:\n- (No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned.)"}
{"ID": "soap_00088", "dialogue": "Good afternoon, sir. Good afternoon, doctor. You're forty four, correct? Yes sir. Great, what seems to be the problem today, sir? Well, doctor, I've had lower back pain for a while now. Does the pain go into your legs? Yeah, I have pain in my right thigh too. Is there an injury associated with this pain? Yes, I had an incident back in nineteen ninety four. Do you have any paperwork or medical records from the initial injury? No sir, I don't have those today. What do you do for a living, sir? Right now, I work at Taco Bell. I have a workers comp claim open. Your pain came back while working there, correct? Yes, that's correct. Do you remember when your last appointment here was? Um, yes, it was on April twelfth two thousand five. With ten being the worst pain imaginable, what was your pain out of ten at your last visit? Um, I'd say it was an eight out of ten. Have you taken any medicine for this? Um, I had a Medrol Dosepak prescribed at my last visit. How did your pain respond to the Dosepak? It took it down to about a four or five out of ten. Can you point to me where the pain is, sir? Yes, it's right here. This band right here? Yeah, that's the spot. Okay, this is between your L four and sacrum. How would you describe your right leg pain? I'd say it's intermittent and minimal now, it's not always there. Have you ever had back surgery? Um, yes, I've had two laminectomies, one in nineteen ninety and the other was in ninety four. Wait, I also had a discectomy some time in between. Do you know where the focus was? It was at L four L 5. Have you had any imaging done on the lower back? Yes, I had an M R I on October eighteenth two thousand four. I have the report right here. Okay, this shows multi level degenerative changes, with facet involvement at L two L three, L three L four, and L five S1, with no neural impingement, which is good. What does that mean, doctor? In summary, you have a significant amount of arthritis in the back. Oh yeah, I had an M R myelogram as well, here's the report. Okay, this shows severe spinal stenosis at L three L four, but it may have been artifact. What does that mean? This finding may be a misinterpretation.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Lower back pain, pain in the right thigh.\n- Severity: Pain rated 8 out of 10 at the last visit, currently down to 4 or 5 out of 10 after taking Medrol Dosepak.\n- Duration: Pain has been present for a while; specific duration not mentioned.\n- Associated symptoms: Intermittent and minimal right leg pain; no current mention of additional symptoms.\n- Relevant medical history: \n - History of two laminectomies (1990 and 1994) and a discectomy between those dates.\n - Previous MRI on October 18, 2004, showing multi-level degenerative changes with facet involvement and no neural impingement.\n - Prior myelogram showing severe spinal stenosis at L3-L4, but possibly an artifact.\n- Family history: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Other concerns: Patient is currently working at Taco Bell, has an open workers' compensation claim, and reports that pain recurred while working.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- Current interpretation includes significant arthritis in the back, as indicated by imaging findings.\n\nPlan:\n- No specific plan mentioned in the conversation."}
{"ID": "soap_00068", "dialogue": "When did the nausea and vomiting start? About a few hours ago. I can't seem to stomach anything. How many episodes of vomiting have you had? At least four. Any abdominal pain, fever, chill, or other symptoms? Just nausea and vomiting. It's been so terrible. I'll order you some Zofran to help bring the nausea to bay. One moment while I put the order in.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Nausea and vomiting\n- Severity: Terrible\n- Duration: A few hours\n- Associated symptoms: None mentioned (no abdominal pain, fever, or chills)\n- Relevant medical history: None provided\n- Family history: None provided\n- Allergies: None mentioned\n- Other concerns: Unable to stomach anything, multiple episodes of vomiting (at least four)\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation, diagnoses, or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- Medication: Order for Zofran to help alleviate nausea."}
{"ID": "soap_00071", "dialogue": "Hi there, I was just hoping to ask you a few questions today but I was wondering if you could tell me what brought you in today. I am just calling about my daughter. She's been sick over the last few days. She has fever and a cough. I see, OK. When did the fever and cough start? So they started around three to four days ago. And around the same time she was also having like a runny nose. OK, and has the fever and cough been present the whole time? Uh, yeah I would say they started around the same time and ah, they have been going on for the last three and a half to four days. OK, so has there been a period when she hasn't had a fever or a cough? Uh no, every time I've measured it, she has had a fever and she's been coughing for the last few days. Hasn't really changed. OK, can you think of anything leading up to these symptoms? Uh, no, she was she was fine, just three to four days ago. Like before that she was her playful self. No issues. But yeah, it's for the last couple of days just this nonstop cough and the fevers is what's really worrying me. OK, uh, have the symptoms have gotten worse or stayed the same? I would say the fever has been getting a little bit worse over the last like, especially today, but the cough, coughing is about the same as what it was when it first started three days ago. OK. Has she been sick like this before? She's, she often gets sick, like her brother goes to daycare and he brings a lot of bugs from there and every time he gets sick, she also gets sick. The last time she had like a similar cough was OK, um, have you, has there been anything that you've tried to help with the cough or fever? Uh Tylenol, I gave her Tylenol. It did help her a little bit, but then the fever came right back, uhm say like a few hours later. OK, and anything you notice that's made the cough or fever worse over the last three or four days? Not anything that I know of. Her fevers been getting worse, but I don't really know why that is. OK, uh, has she been having any other symptoms? Um, other than like, yeah, she had that runny nose when it first started, and then she's also been having some diarrhea. OK, and when did the diarrhea start? I think I would say it started around two days ago. She's, like every time I change her diaper she would have like 4-5 different diaper changes throughout the day, and they they seem pretty liquid. So I OK, uh you mentioned liquid appearing stool, what would you say say, so I guess, could you comment on the consistency and colour? Consistency, I'd say it's pretty watery, like diarrhea and colour I didn't really think there's any changes in in the colour. OK. So you didn't think there was any blood in the stool? No, no, no, I didn't see any blood. OK, as for the fever, what was the maximum temperature that you noted and how did you check it? So I used, we have just a regular non digital thermometer in her ear and her temperature today was around 103 degrees Fahrenheit. OK. Uh, did you notice any tugging or pulling at her ear? No, no, she doesn't seem to have any irritation there. OK, uh, how about any vomiting or regurgitation? No, she's been keeping food down. No issues there. OK, and notice any discharge from either eye? Uh, no. How about any discharge from ears? Not that I've noticed, no. Notice any rashes? Uh, no, I didn't not know like new rashes, no. OK and how is she been in terms of mood or or behaviour? Oh she's been pretty like she's not herself over the last few days. She's been crying a lot more, just very irritable. OK, would you say inconsolable at times? Sometimes like yesterday like she, I couldn't get her to stop crying. OK and uh, I know you said no vomiting, but has she been coughing up any sputum? Yeah, I would say, uh, this green slimy content. Yeah, it's like green and slimy. OK, um, how much would you say, uhm, like a cup, or more? I would say probably like at least, at least a couple spoonfuls at a time, she would cough up. OK, and did you notice any blood in it? No, I didn't notice any blood at all. OK, and with the cough, have you noticed any difficulties breathing? Uh, I don't know if it's difficulty breathing, but I have noticed sometimes she's like breathing faster than normal, like taking a lot more breaths. I see, OK. Uh, is she having troubles with drooling or your trouble speaking? Uh, no. I haven't noticed that. OK, and does her voice sound normal to you? Yeah, I think her note, her voice hasn't really changed that much. OK, um, with the noticing the faster rate of breathing is the breathing noisy at all? Uh, no, like she's not wheezing or anything. OK, and do you notice any like retraction of the skin between her ribs when breathing. Uh so like, uhm, like what do you mean by that? Like just so when she's breathing in and out. Is she generating a lot of force and effort to really kind of suck in and blow out so you can kind of see impressions over the ribs. Uh no, I I don't think I've noticed that, no. OK. And, and, have you noticed any blueish discolouration around her lips or mouth? Uh, no. OK, and any urinary problems? Uh, no, she's peeing fine. OK. And how has she been sleeping the last few days? She hasn't really been sleeping in between the crying and like just being so irritable. I think she's just very uncomfortable when she's trying to get to sleep, so OK. Any evidence of seizure or limb jerking? Uh no, not that I've noticed. No, OK. And and have you notice an her being like really lethargic at all? Uh, she's not herself and she's still like pretty active, like she's, she just seems really unhappy and irritated. But no, she still has energy. OK. And uh, is she drinking yeah, a lot more fluids or or a lot less? How is she tolerating oral intake? I would say I think she's more on the dryer side like she, her mouth is very dry. Her lips are like all cracked, and she it seems that she's drinking I see, OK. And, uh, how many wet diapers would you say in the last 24 hours? Um? Yeah, it's difficult to say. I think I like yesterday changed four to five times. They were all diarrhea, so not really sure, but maybe around 5. OK, and have you guys traveled anywhere recently? No. No, we've been here. OK, so next just some questions about past medical history. Uh, has she had any medical problems in the past? The only thing I could think of was after she was born, she had to be given the light treatment for like jaundice, but after that no other problems. OK. And any prior surgeries? No. Any previous hospitalizations? No, just the light treatment, but other than that nothing. OK, and throughout pregnancy were there any complications? No, my pregnancy was completely normal. No complications during the pregnancy. OK, so no gestational diabetes or hypertension? Uh, no. OK and uh, were you sick at all at any point with infectious symptoms? No, everything came back clear. All the tests were normal. OK, and um. Any allergies to medications? No allergies. OK, and is she currently taking any medications? No, just tried the Tylenol but it didn't really seem to work. OK, and has she been around anybody sick recently? Just her big brother who goes to daycare was also like a bit sick with a cough and some runny nose for the last four days. But, uhm, he didn't have a fever and he seemed to recover pretty fast but seems she may have caught it from him. OK, and anybody in the family um, have any similar symptoms right now? I guess besides her brother? No, just just her and her brother. OK. And, and in regards to birth, birth history, was it a vaginal delivery or C-section? It was a normal vaginal delivery at 38 weeks there were no problems after the delivery or before the delivery. OK, and uh are her immunizations up to date? Yeah uh, all our immunizations are up to date. OK, and um, in regards to growth and development, have you had any concerns? Uh, no, we've been getting her checked regularly. It seems that she's been growing well and developing well. OK, and who is the primary caregiver for at home? Primary caregiver, right now I'm staying home with her and her brother goes to daycare. So yeah, it would be me. OK. Uh, and could you tell me about her eating habits and typical appetite? Uh, so now like she is able to eat like everything like vegetables, fruits, breads and meat, but over the last few days she has been just irritable with everything. So even eating food has just not been great for the last few days. OK, and when was her last checkup? So it was around one month ago and everything was fine then. No concerns from the doctor. OK. Thank you.", "note": "Subjective:\n- 3 yo F presents with fever and cough x 3-4 days\n- Fever: Tmax 103\u00b0F (ear), persistent, worsening\n- Cough: Persistent, productive with green slimy sputum (couple spoonfuls), no blood\n- Associated sx: Runny nose, diarrhea x 2 days (watery, 4-5 episodes/day, no blood), irritability, \u2193 PO intake, \u2193 sleep\n- Denies: Ear tugging/pain, vomiting, eye/ear discharge, rashes, difficulty breathing/wheezing, voice changes, urinary sx, seizures\n- Tylenol tried with temporary improvement in fever\n- No known worsening/alleviating factors\n- Similar cough 2 months ago without fever\n- Exposed to sick brother (cough, runny nose x 4 days) who attends daycare\n\nPMHx:\n- Neonatal jaundice requiring phototherapy\n- Up-to-date on immunizations\n- No other medical conditions, surgeries, hospitalizations, or allergies\n\nBirth Hx: SVD at 38 weeks, uncomplicated\n\nSocHx:\n- Mother is primary caregiver\n- Brother attends daycare\n\nObjective:\n- Vitals: Not provided\n- Exam: Not performed\n\nAssessment:\n- Likely viral URTI\n\nPlan:\n- Continue supportive care with Tylenol, fluids, rest\n- Monitor for signs of respiratory distress, dehydration, or worsening symptoms\n- Follow-up with PCP if not improving in 2-3 days or sooner if worsening"}
{"ID": "soap_00031", "dialogue": "Hello, are you translating for the patient today? I am sorry, but I do not speak Spanish. Guest_family: Yes doctor. My chart says this man is fifty two years old, is all of that correct? Guest_family: Yes doctor, that's correct. Good, so, what seems to be the problem today? Guest_family: He hurt himself a while back when he slipped on a grape while he was at the grocery store. Oh no, when was this? Guest_family: It was on October second two thousand eight. Which knee did he injure? Guest_family: Doctor, it was his left knee. How has he been treated for this so far? Guest_family: He went straight to the E D immediately, and then he saw his P C P. What did the P C P recommend? Guest_family: He only referred him to P T, and gave him a knee brace.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is a 52-year-old male.\n- He slipped on a grape at the grocery store, injuring his left knee.\n- Injury date: October 2, 2008.\n- Treatment received: Immediate care at the emergency department, followed by referral to physical therapy and provision of a knee brace by his primary care physician.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation, diagnoses, or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00017", "dialogue": "Are you married, ma'am? Yes, to my wonderful husband. Does he help you out around the house? Yes, he is just wonderful. So, do you drink or smoke? No, I don't do any of that. How about more illicit substances, do you use any of that? No, absolutely not.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is married.\n- Expresses positive feelings towards husband, describing him as \"wonderful.\"\n- Denies alcohol use.\n- Denies smoking.\n- Denies use of illicit substances.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation, diagnoses, or clinical impressions provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00033", "dialogue": "Do you smoke cigarettes? Yes. Do you drink alcohol or use recreational drugs? Yes, I have been in and out of treatment. I am not doing so well with staying clean. What substances do you use? I drink, and I did some coke today. I have been in treatment for heroin and alcohol.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient smokes cigarettes.\n- Patient drinks alcohol and has used cocaine today.\n- Patient has a history of treatment for heroin and alcohol.\n- Patient expresses difficulty in staying clean.\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data provided.]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No clinical interpretation or diagnoses provided.]\n\nPlan:\n- [No specific actions, medications, tests, or follow-up mentioned.]"}
{"ID": "soap_00018", "dialogue": "What brings you in? Hi, I've I've had this pain on the outside of my uh right elbow now it's it I first started knowing, noticing it several months ago, but recently it's just been more painful. OK, so you said several months ago. Um did anything happen several months ago? Was there any sort of trigger, trauma, anything like that to that area? No, there wasn't any any trauma or any triggers that that I noticed, I was just um feeling it, uh, a bit more at the end of of work. Um yeah, I was just having it uh, feeling the pain a bit more at the end of work. OK, does uh anything make it better or worse, the pain? Um yeah, if I, really if I'm just resting the elbow um it makes it better, and I've tried uh things like ibuprofen um which has helped with the pain, I'll I'll do that for um hoping I can get through work sometimes if the pain is bad enough. Right, OK. Um and if you were to describe the quality of the pain, is it sharp, throbby, achy? Uh it's um kind of uh, well, it's achy and then sometimes depending on the movement it can get, it can be sharp as well. It can be sharp, OK. OK, um and what sorts of movements make it worse? Um, so like, really it's mostly the movements at my wrist, if I'm bending my wrist down, uh I can I can feel it, or um if I'm having to pick things up or hold heavy objects at work, I do a lot of repetitive uh things at at work, I work on a line. OK, OK. And 1 to 10, 10 being the worst pain you've ever felt, how bad is this pain? It is about a four. About a four, OK. And have you ever experienced this type of pain before? Uh, no I haven't. No, OK, and do you feel like your pain is getting worse over the past several months, or is it the same, getting better? Uh it's been getting worse. It's been getting worse, OK. OK, um have you tried any like topical ointments or anything like that for it? Uh I did, I yeah, I tried some um some stuff that you can get at the um drugstore and it did help take the edge off a little bit. OK. Um alright and are you still able to move that arm, your right arm, um as well as your hand? I am, yeah, I I have full range of motion of both the elbow and uh hand. OK and it's not like preventing you from doing everyday tasks? Uh, no. I I've been been able to manage so far at work, but um it is starting to become an issue um yeah, with my job. OK. Um alright, and then do you have any sorts of aches or pains uh in any other joint or muscle groups? No, it's just right there on the outside of my right elbow, and it's really annoying 'cause I'm right handed as well, so um yeah, it's causing problems there. Right, um and is the pain radiating upwards or downwards? Uh no, I kinda just feel it right over that that one spot. OK, any numbness or tingling in that arm? No, no, no numbness or tingling. Yeah, um OK, and then how about um like your arm feeling like it's cold? No, I haven't had anything like that. OK, alright, um and then otherwise do you have any other symptoms? Um no I, it's just been the elbow pain. OK, so no weight loss? No, my weights been stable over these last few months. OK, fevers or chills? No, I haven't had any fevers or chills. OK, any fatigue? No, my energy's been good. OK. How about um rashes? Uh no rashes. OK. Changes to your senses like vision, hearing, smell, smell or taste? No changes there. OK any coughing, shortness of breath, or wheezing? No. OK. Chest pain, any heart palpitations, or uh dizziness? Nothing like that. OK, changes to your bowel movements? No. No, no no nausea or vomiting? No, I haven't had any nausea or vomiting. OK. Changes to your urine? No, I haven't had any urinary uh symptoms. OK. Memory loss or confusion? No. OK, so if you look at that right elbow, um do you notice any redness or swelling? Uh no, I don't. No, OK. Does it seem misaligned or like asymmetrical compared to your other elbow? No, it looks, it looks the same. Looks the same, OK so no um skin changes or deformities there? Uh no. No, OK, alright and now I'm going to get you to um just palpate uh that lateral or yeah, the lateral aspect of that elbow and you let me know if you feel pain when you push, push on that. Uh there is pain at the lateral uh lateral elbow. How about the medial aspect? No pain. No pain, OK, and how about when you palpate your um forearm and your biceps? Uh no no pain. OK, alright and then do you feel like it's hot, that area? No. No, OK now I am going to get you to um extend your wrist. OK. And then keep your elbow in full extension as well. Does that hurt? Uh it does, yeah. Yeah, OK, how about when you flex your wrist with the elbow still in full extension? Uh it does, yeah that hurts as well. OK, how about when you flex your elbow? Uh no, that that doesn't cause any pain. OK. How about when you extend your shoulder all the way up? No, nothing there, that feels fine. OK, and then flexing your uh, or sorry abducting your shoulder? That feels normal. OK, alright, and then how about when you pronate and supinate your forearms? Uh, pronation um? So like twist your um arm inwards so that your palm is facing the floor. No, that doesn't really cause any pain, pronation and supination. OK, alright. Um alright, and then are you otherwise healthy or do you have any longstanding medical conditions? Um I'm uh healthy. You're healthy, OK. And then UM do you have, have you had any hospitalizations or surgeries in the past? I had a surgery for um a C-section for uh my, my son, um but that's that's it. OK. OK, and um how long ago was that? That was about uh 17 years ago. OK. And was that an emergency C-section? Uh no, it was not. OK um and then are you on any medications? Um no, I've just been taking uh Tylenol and Advil um for the pain. OK, alright um immunizations wise, are you up to date? Uh I I am, yes. OK. Do you have any allergies? Uh no allergies. OK, family history of any uh skeletal or muscular um issues? Um no, nothing like that in the family. OK, neurological issues in the family? Um no neurological issues in the family. OK, how about um autoimmune conditions? Uh no, nothing like that. OK and then um do you smoke? Uh no, I haven't smoked before. OK, uh do you do um marijuana. No, I I don't use that. OK. Um Alcohol? Um uh yeah, I'll have a glass of wine every once in awhile, but maybe just one, one a week. OK, and have you been around sick contacts recently? Um no, I haven't been around anybody who's been sick. OK. Any recent travel? Uh nope, I've been at home. OK. Um I think those are all the questions that I had for you, um did you have any questions of your own for myself? Uh no, I was just wondering what this pain could be on my, in my elbow. Yeah, so right now it seems like you may have a case of something called um lateral epicondylitis. So, it's also known as uh tennis elbow, um and there are a lot of things that could kind of predispose you to this, um so it could be like the repetitive strain at work, um and like continuous contraction of your wrist extensor muscle group there. Um so for this we basically recommend um a couple of things to manage the pain. Uh but first, like in terms of imaging, I don't think it is necessary because it doesn't seem like there was any trauma and I'm not too worried about any fractures. Um so to help you treat this, um we recommend some activity modifications, so um at work not doing things that are too repetitive, especially if it's using those um extensor forearm muscles. Um and then you can use some, use a compression sleeve as well, continue taking Tylenol or NSAIDs for the pain and uh really important is physical therapy um to increase mobility and strength in that arm. Uh and as well you can apply things like ice um to it for like the pain and swelling if you see any. Uh, yeah, so it should resolve in about six months to two years at most, uh but normally it does um go away in a couple of months with some physical therapy and the compression and splints if you choose to do that. How does that sound? Yeah, that that sounds um good, thank you. Yeah, no worries.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Right elbow pain, lateral aspect, started several months ago, recently more painful (4/10 severity)\n- No trauma or triggers noted, pain worse at end of work day\n- Pain improves with rest and ibuprofen\n- Pain is achy, sometimes sharp with certain movements (wrist flexion, lifting/holding heavy objects at work)\n- Repetitive work on a line\n- Topical OTC ointments provide some relief\n- Able to move right arm and hand fully, but pain is becoming an issue at work\n- Pain localized to lateral right elbow, no radiation\n- No numbness, tingling, or coldness in arm\n\nObjective:\n- No redness, swelling, misalignment, or deformity of right elbow\n- Tenderness to palpation over lateral elbow, no tenderness over medial elbow, forearm, or biceps\n- Pain with wrist extension and flexion, elbow in full extension\n- No pain with elbow flexion, shoulder extension, adduction, forearm pronation, or supination\n\nPast Medical History:\n- Healthy, no chronic medical conditions\n- C-section 17 years ago (non-emergent)\n- No medications other than Tylenol and Advil PRN for pain\n- Up to date on immunizations\n- No allergies\n- No family history of skeletal, muscular, neurological, or autoimmune conditions\n\nSocial History:\n- Non-smoker, no marijuana use\n- Occasional alcohol use (1 glass of wine per week)\n- No recent sick contacts or travel\n\nAssessment:\n- Lateral epicondylitis (tennis elbow)\n\nPlan:\n- No imaging necessary at this time\n- Activity modification at work to avoid repetitive motions and heavy lifting\n- Consider using compression sleeve\n- Continue Tylenol or NSAIDs PRN for pain\n- Physical therapy for mobility and strengthening\n- Apply ice for pain and swelling PRN\n- Expected resolution in 6 months to 2 years with conservative management"}
{"ID": "soap_00058", "dialogue": "What do you use to treat your asthma? I use my Albuterol inhaler.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient uses Albuterol inhaler to treat asthma.\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data provided]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No assessment provided]\n\nPlan:\n- [No plan provided]"}
{"ID": "soap_00082", "dialogue": "I just met with one of my patients whom I believe has pertussis. Guest_clinician: Second case of the day. What's their name and date of birth? Hm give me a second to pull up their information.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is suspected to have pertussis.\n- No specific symptoms, severity, duration, associated symptoms, relevant medical history, family history, allergies, or concerns were explicitly mentioned by the patient in the conversation.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- Suspected diagnosis: Pertussis.\n\nPlan:\n- No specific actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00050", "dialogue": "Good morning, young lady, how old are you? Good morning, doctor. I'm thirteen. Good, and what seems to be the problem today? Mom, can you explain for me? Guest_family: Well, if you look, doctor, her back posture is very rounded. I think, it's rounding about the thoracic spine. Is there a family history of this problem? Guest_family: Yes, on my side, my aunt and grandfather had, um, kyphosis. Yes, that's what this is. This is thoracic kyphosis to be specific. Has she seen another doctor for this? Guest_family: Yes, we saw another orthopedist. What did they recommend? Guest_family: They recommended we come in for further observation, so we're here for a second opinion. Good, is there any back pain, numbness or tingling? No, I don't have any of that. Is there any weakness, numbness or tingling in your legs and arms, my dear? No, I'm very strong, especially for my age. Are you going to the bathroom with no problem? Yes, doctor, everything is regular there.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is a 13-year-old girl.\n- Complaints of rounded back posture (thoracic spine).\n- Family history of kyphosis (aunt and grandfather).\n- No back pain, numbness, or tingling reported.\n- No weakness, numbness, or tingling in legs and arms.\n- Patient states she feels very strong for her age.\n- Regular bathroom habits reported.\n\nObjective:\n- Observed rounded back posture (thoracic kyphosis).\n\nAssessment:\n- Thoracic kyphosis.\n\nPlan:\n- Further observation as recommended by the previous orthopedist.\n- Consideration for a second opinion on management or treatment options."}
{"ID": "soap_00063", "dialogue": "Hello, miss. The nurse who seated you tells me you are here for a second opinion on surgery. Hello, doctor. Yes, I have been told that both eyes need cataract extraction. I came for a second opinion because I will only have surgery if necessary. I am nervous. What is the reason for the extraction? I am not exactly sure because the other doctors said a bunch of medical terms. I have had laser in my eyes though. That was for macular degeneration. Doctor X said I had neurovascular age-related macular degeneration. Do you know what laser you had on your eyes? No. I just know laser was done by Doctor X four times to the macula on the right and two times on the left.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is seeking a second opinion on surgery.\n- Concerned about needing cataract extraction in both eyes.\n- Patient is nervous about the surgery.\n- Previous medical history includes laser treatment for macular degeneration.\n- Doctor X diagnosed the patient with neurovascular age-related macular degeneration.\n- The patient underwent laser treatment four times on the right macula and two times on the left.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided in the conversation.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinical interpretations, diagnoses, or differential diagnoses provided in the conversation.\n\nPlan:\n- No specific actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education provided in the conversation."}
{"ID": "soap_00083", "dialogue": "Are you allergic to anything? Yes, I am allergic to eggs and peanuts. Oh, okay. Anything else? Any medication? No, nothing else. Alright.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Allergies: Eggs and peanuts.\n- No other allergies or medications mentioned.\n\nObjective:\n- [No objective data provided.]\n\nAssessment:\n- [No assessment information provided.]\n\nPlan:\n- [No plan provided.]"}
{"ID": "soap_00099", "dialogue": "What happened to you? How did you get involved in this accident? I was just trying to adjust cable that my brother's dog was tied to. All of a sudden, he attacked me out of nowhere. The injury to my leg is V shaped and the V is pointing towards my foot. What kind of dog? German shepherd. But he is up to date on his shots, and he is completely healthy. He bit me on my foot, and it looks like his teeth went into my skin. I went to my P C P. What did you do there? They cleaned the wound and did some bandage along with it he gave me a tetanus shot. And he sent you to us? Yes. Okay let me examine your wound. Sure! It looks like there is a flap injury developed after puncture wound. It seems like a centimeter long and viable. How old are you sir? And what ethnicity do you identify with? White male. I am fifty years old. Is my right leg going to be okay? The skin looks viable. We can save the tissue.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient was attacked by a German shepherd while adjusting a cable.\n- Describes injury to leg as V shaped, pointing towards the foot.\n- Reports that the dog is healthy and up to date on vaccinations.\n- Injury is described as a bite on the foot, with the dog's teeth penetrating the skin.\n- Patient previously visited primary care physician (PCP) who cleaned the wound, applied a bandage, and administered a tetanus shot.\n- Patient expresses concern about the condition of the right leg.\n\nObjective:\n- Wound examination reveals a flap injury developed from a puncture wound.\n- Flap injury is approximately 1 centimeter long and appears viable.\n\nAssessment:\n- Puncture wound from dog bite to the foot with viable tissue.\n\nPlan:\n- Further evaluation and management of the wound to ensure proper healing and tissue viability."}
{"ID": "soap_00040", "dialogue": "Can you tell me what brought you in here today? Sure, I've been feeling very short of breath for the past, uh, two weeks, but even before that, I started feeling a little bit short of breath and I can't stop coughing and it just feels like it's really hard for me to breathe. OK, so this started two weeks ago? Yeah, it started two weeks ago. OK, and you're also coughing? Yeah. OK, and how long have you been coughing for? The same amount of time, it started about, maybe three weeks ago, I started coughing more, um, but now it's worse. OK, and what kind of cough are you having? Is it a wet cough or a dry cough? It's definitely wet. My phlegm looks green. OK, and how long have you noticed it being green? It's been like that for the past two weeks. OK, and before that, were you having coughing as well? Yeah, before that I was still coughing but not as much. OK, uhm, and was there anything that happened, uh, did you get sick at all recently, or were you in contact with anyone who was sick? No, not that I know of. OK, any kind of fevers at all? No, no fevers. OK. Have you noticed any kind of, uh, coughing up blood or blood tinged sputum at all? Uhm no not that, no blood. OK, and how much sputum are you bringing up in an average day now? Uhm, I'm bringing up about, I'd say it would fill like two, two of my palms, like the inside of my palm. OK. Alright, and is your coughing like constant or does it come and go? It's pretty constant now. OK. And have you had at all any kind of, uh, fevers or anything like that? No fevers. OK, and how about your shortness of breath? Is that, when does that happen? Is it mainly with exercise and exertion, or can it happen even when you're just going on about your day? Uhm, its, uh, ok, its more with exertion, but right now even at rest, I'm coughing and feeling short of breath. OK. And are you feeling at all any kind of, uh, chest tightness or pain? Uh, yeah, some chest tightness. And is that mainly when you're coughing and short of breath or, uhm, at other times as well? Uhm, other times as well. OK. Are you having any other, any other like, can you describe your chest pain a little bit? Is it kind of a pressure pain or is it a sharp pain, aching pain? How would you describe it? Uhm, I would say it's a, uh, it's a tightness in my chest. And it feels, I guess throbbing or achy? OK, and does this pain, uhm, move to anywhere else or does it just stay in your chest? Uh, it just stays in my chest. OK, have you been feeling dizzy or have you fainted at all? Uh, no. Have you been feeling increased sweating and confusion? Anything like that? No. OK, have you felt your heart racing and anything like that? No. OK. Alright, and is there anything that makes your symptoms better or worse? Uhm, I guess activity makes it worse, un, and like cold air, things like that. OK. And other than that, have you recently at all traveled outside of the country or outside of the province? Uh, no. No, not recently. OK, now are you having any wheezing at all? Uhm, some wheezing, yeah. OK. And any kind of headaches or nausea or vomiting. No, nothing like that. OK, and any fevers or chills. Ah, nope, nothing like that. Any night sweats? Ah, sometimes I feel sweaty, but that's just with the shortness of breath. OK, and how long does your shortness of breath usually last? It lasts about, uh, I don't know, I kind of feel short of breath all the time, but when it's really bad, it lasts about five-ten minutes and then it goes back to like my normal shortness of breath. OK. And has this happened to you before? Uhm no, I don't think so. OK. Like not, not this bad, I've always had some shortness of breath but nothing like this. OK. And , uh, do you have any kind of diarrhea or constipation? Uhm, no nothing like that. OK, how about fatigue? Yeah, I feel fatigued. OK, and is that just about today or is it, do you think it's because of your shortness of breath? Yeah, it's probably cause of my shortness of breath. Are you having any of these symptoms when you're trying to sleep? uh yeah, I think its, yeah when I'm laying down sometimes I feel more short of breath than usual, but once I shift positions it goes back to it's normal. Ok. Is it, do you ever have to wake up at night to catch a breath or anything like that? Ah, no, it doesn't wake me up. And have you noticed any kind of, uh, significant weight loss or anything like that recently? No. OK, and have you at all had any changes in your appetite or anything like that? No, no change in appetite. OK. Any exposures to any like dust, fumes or any irritants recently No. OK And, in the past, have you been diagnosed with any medical conditions? Uhm, just COPD, uhm, and, I also have some congestive heart failure. OK, and when were you diagnosed with both of them? COPD I've been, I got diagnosed about seven years ago, CHF about two years ago. OK, and uh, right now do you have any symptoms of any swelling in your extremities like legs, hands or anything like that? Some swelling in, some swelling in my legs, yeah? OK, and do you have any kind of shortness of breath when you're lying down, at all? Uh, a little bit. Yeah, a little bit I would say. OK. And any other medical conditions? Nope, no others. Any allergies? No. Any medications you're on right now? Yeah I'm on, uh, an ACE inhibitor, I'm on, uh, Spironolactone, uhm, I'm on, I have some puffers for my COPD. I believe, what do I have, I have, um, I have a long acting beta agonist. And I guess I have a short acting as well for when I need it. OK. And do you have, so is it just the two, two puffers? Mhm. OK, and are you using, like the blue one, the blue one more at all, are you having to use the short acting one more? I'm definitely using the short acting more, like I'm using it almost every day. And does it help at all? Uh I4 think so. OK yeah, so other than that, are you on any other medications? No. No, OK. And have you been taking your Spironolactone? Yeah, I've been taking all my medications. OK, any previous hospitalizations or surgeries? I was hospitalised two years ago for shortness of breath and some low oxygen, that's when they diagnosed me with CHF. I see OK and did they say at all, It was caused by, like your COPD or what was it caused by? Do you remember? Uhm, I guess just, yeah, I'm not really too sure, they said I had some heart condition and my heart isn't functioning as well as it should be. They did an echo on me. OK, and have you at all had any kind of heart attacks or anything like that? No. No, OK. and did they, when you were having shortness of breath, did you have a like a pneumonia or infection that you had to get antibiotics for any or anything like that? Uhm, no. Actually I might have gotten some antibiotics at one point, but not during that visit. OK. OK, And currently right now do you smoke? No, I stopped smoking two years ago. Yeah no, that's really good. And definitely with these symptoms and your condition that definitely helps to quit smoking whenever you can, so congratulations for that. Any alcohol at all? I drink some alcohol. I drink maybe once a week. OK, and any recreational drugs like marijuana? I smoke once in a while. OK, how often would you say you smoke marijuana? I smoke about a joint a week. OK, and uh, do you use a, like the provincial marijuana, or do you have any other source, what would you say you use? I use uhm, Pardon me, what was that? Do I use? Like uh, do you get it from a store that sells the joint? Yeah, I get it from like a medical dispensary. OK, OK, I see. Yeah, I just want to make sure because some, if you get it from other sources there might be more irritants in there that can cause some of your symptoms and for them to get worse as well. So yeah. Oh I see, I see, OK. Yeah, and other than that, do you have any questions or concerns at all? No, what do you think I should do from, For now? Yeah, so right now what we're going to do is because you're having the coughing, shortness of breath, and you have a history of COPD & CHF, we definitely want to make sure that you're not having, uhm, what I think you might be having isn't just an exacerbation of your COPD, and especially with the green sputum it might be like a bacterial infection that's causing your symptoms to get worse, so we'll do all the appropriate testing, we'll do, a chest X-ray, we'll get some blood work done as well, w'ell give you some uh, Ventolin as well while you're here, and we might give you like a steroid medication as well. So we'll do some of those things right now for you to kind of help you right now, as well as do some of those tests that I mentioned. Depending on what comes back, you might require an admission to the hospital just because we want to make sure you don't go home with an exacerbation, especially with the CHF as well, we need to, we will need to get that under control because that can get worse with your COPD exacerbation as well. So we need to make sure some of that, swelling goes down, if we need to adjust your medications while you're here. So yeah, let's just, I'll get back to you, we'll do some of those testing and then see where to go from there. Is that OK? Yeah, that sounds great. Thank you. You're welcome.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Presenting with shortness of breath for 2 weeks, worsening cough for 3 weeks\n- Cough: productive with green sputum for 2 weeks, constant\n- Shortness of breath: worse with exertion, cold air; present at rest; lasts 5-10 minutes when severe before returning to baseline\n- Chest tightness/pain: aching, throbbing; localized to chest\n- Denies: fevers, chills, hemoptysis, dizziness, syncope, diaphoresis, confusion, palpitations, headache, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, constipation, significant weight loss, appetite changes, dust/fume/irritant exposure\n- Mild lower extremity edema\n- Mild orthopnea\n- Fatigue\n- Dyspnea worsens when supine, improves with position changes\n\nPMHx:\n- COPD (diagnosed 7 years ago)\n- CHF (diagnosed 2 years ago)\n- Hospitalized 2 years ago for shortness of breath and hypoxemia, diagnosed with CHF\n\nMedications:\n- ACE inhibitor\n- Spironolactone\n- LABA inhaler\n- SABA inhaler - increased use, using almost daily, provides some relief\n\nSocHx:\n- Former smoker (quit 2 years ago)\n- Drinks alcohol once weekly\n- Smokes marijuana (1 joint/week) obtained from medical dispensary\n\nObjective:\n- Increased work of breathing\n\nAssessment:\n- Likely COPD exacerbation\n- Possible bacterial infection given green sputum production\n\nPlan:\n- CXR\n- Labs\n- Ventolin in ED\n- Consider steroids\n- May require admission for COPD exacerbation management and CHF optimization\n- Adjust medications as needed"}
{"ID": "soap_00091", "dialogue": "So what brings you in? Yeah, so um I just came in because uh so I I have this cough that's been going on for the last five years, but over the last month it's really been getting worse. Um and like last week I I've just been constantly coughing. So yeah, I was just a bit concerned. Yeah, OK, so you said the past couple of years you've been coughing. So how many years exactly would that be? Two, three, four? Probably five I would say, and I was diagnosed with uh COPD around that time. OK, OK, um the cough that you currently have, um is it a dry cough or are you bringing up any phlegm? Uh I'm pretty much always bringing up like this uh white material. White, OK. Um and is it constant throughout the day or do you find it comes and goes? It's pretty much there all the time, but uh it's definitely getting worse. Um it is, I think, the worst in the morning um when I wake up and I just feel like there's just a bunch of gunk stuck in the back of my throat. OK. Um and if you were to describe the quality of this cough, would you say it's harsh, barky, muffled? I would say it's harsh. Harsh, OK. And has the quality of your cough changed at all in the past few years? Like does it sound different than it used to? Um I don't know if it sounds different, but uh especially for the last month I would say I'm bringing up more of the material, and it's usually white over the five years, but um I noticed uh in the last week-ish, it's had a yellowish tinge to it as well. OK, OK, so it's changed a little bit. Um and are you experiencing any shortness of breath with this? No I I don't think so, like I sometimes when I'm walking around uh doing more stuff, uh working in the garden like I feel like I might have to breathe a little bit harder, but uh not regularly. OK, OK, that's good. Um and any wheezing? Uh, yeah. It's like usually I start coughing a lot more when I'm starting to do any kind of activities and sometimes when I start coughing more, I also start wheezing. Um but uh right now, I'm fine. You're fine now, OK, uh, alright. And sorry, forgot to ask you earlier, do you see any blood in your phlegm? No, no, I haven't noticed any blood. OK, any uh changes to your hair or on your skin, like rashes? Not that I've noticed, no. OK, any weight loss? Um, I don't think so. OK, fevers? No. OK, exhaustion? Um yeah, I would say like for the last few months I am a bit more tired. Mhmm OK, how about changes in your vision or hearing? No, I haven't experienced that. How about a stuffy nose? Runny nose? Uh no, not really. No, OK. How about changes to your smell or taste? Uh nope. No, OK. Uh chest pain? Not chest pain, but when I get into those coughing fits, sometimes it feels just really tight in my chest. Oh, yeah. But it tends to go away with uh just some rest. Oh OK, OK. Um how about palpitations? Like feeling like your heart is racing? Uh no, I don't think so. No, OK. How about some swelling in your limbs? I I did notice, like uh a little bit of swelling in my uh both my, like right by my ankles. My shoes, like like even when I wear my socks, it like leaves little marks and then uh I just feel my like my shoes are tighter. OK, OK and how long has that been going on? Probably, uh, I've had this issue for like a pretty long time um, probably I would say the last year at least. OK, OK, last year. Um any uh dizziness or fainting spells? No. No, OK, any memory issues, uh confusion? No. Numbness or tingling? No, nothing like that. No headaches? Uh no, no headaches. Changes to your bowel movements? No, I tend to go like once a day. And yeah, it's pretty normal. OK, uh nausea or vomiting? No. No, OK. And are you otherwise healthy? Do you have any longstanding conditions? I know you said you have COPD, anything else? I do have high blood pressure. OK. Do you have something called congestive heart failure? Uh no, what's that? OK, it's when um your heart's not pumping as much as it should be, um so you get some backup of fluid. I ask because you you mentioned that you um were having some swelling in your legs. Oh I yeah, I I yeah, I didn't know anything about that. I just thought that was uh, it's been going on for a while now and didn't really bother me too much. OK. Um alright, and are you taking any medications? Yeah, I have this medication uh right now I'm taking it, it's called lisinopril. OK, so that's for your blood pressure? Yeah. OK. Are you on any puffers? Sorry, go ahead. Yes I'm on, no worries, I'm on um uh two puffers right now. One of them is like just one that I use when I really need it. And then um there's another one, so I think that's like the Ventolin puffer. Yeah. And then the other one is called, I think it's called Spiriva. OK, OK perfect. And are your puff puffers giving you any relief at all from the coughing fits? Yeah, I've been using the Ventolin uh more and it it does seem to help. OK, OK. Uh do you find they're not working as much as they used to though? Yeah, I feel like uh before, I didn't really like, it, just last year I didn't really have to take it too much. Um like even when I use the puffer, I start getting coughing like maybe half an hour later and so it's not, I don't think it's as effective. OK, yeah. Um OK. And have you had any recent blood work done? Uh, no, I haven't been to the doctor for a while. OK. And do you have any allergies? Um I think I have allergies to uh like um amoxicillin, I got like a rash once. OK. Have you had any, uh, amoxicillin, or like penicillin type antibiotics recently? No. No, OK. Um are you up to date with all your immunizations? Yep. OK. Um, you're not quite at this age range yet, but uh we do recommend the pneumococcal vaccine when you turn 65. And um did you get the flu vaccine this year? Uh yeah I did, with the pandemic going on, I usually don't get it, but I got it this year. OK, good good. Um any family history of lung conditions or heart conditions? Uhm, lung conditions I don't think so. Heart conditions, uh my father did have a heart attack in his 60s, but he's fine. He's he's living right now. OK OK, any cancers? Cancers, I think my uncle from my father's side um had uh bladder cancer. OK, OK. Um do you smoke? I used to smoke. Um I just quit a few years ago. OK, congrats. Um how much did you used to smoke? Uhm, I don't know, probably I went through a like a whole pack every day, I would say for at least 30 years of my life. OK, OK um so it's good that you stopped. Um do you take any other recreational drugs? Uh no, nothing like that. No, OK. How about alcohol? Do you drink? Uh I drink every like, maybe a beer a day. OK. Um are you um living with anyone right now? Yeah, it's just uh me and my wife. OK, where do you guys live? Uh we just live uh outside of the city in in a condominium. OK, any uh old carpets? Mold, dust, asbestos, anything like that in the condo? No, it's pretty well maintained. OK, good. And have you been in contact with any anyone sick recently? No, I've mainly just been trying to stay inside. I work from home too, so haven't really been seeing anyone. OK, that's good. And um travel to anywhere recently? Outside the province? Uhm, outside of the province, no, not not for like the last year at least. OK, OK. Um so those were kind of all the questions that I had for you. Did you have any questions for me? Um yeah, I was just wondering like what you think it might be. Yeah, so it definitely sounds like a COPD exacerbation, but because your symptoms overlap with those of Covid, we will take a swab and you should hear back in about 24 to 48 hours. Um and you can you can self isolate for 14 days um and if it's positive, public health will be in touch with you to um do some contact tracing, but in the meantime we'll also do a work up for the COPD exacerbation. Um it seems like you have taken the correct steps so far in like stopping like stopping your smoking and um taking your puffers as needed. Um so right now we will continue using the puffers that you have, and if you don't already have a long acting beta agonist, we'll give you that as well as, if it's like if it gets really severe, we'll also give you some inhaled corticosteroids um and we'll also check your oxygen saturation to make sure it's over uh 88% um and we'll give you oxygen if you need it. Um as well, some antibiotics to consider, you mentioned that you were allergic to amoxicillin, so we might give you like a macrolide like azithromycin for now. Um and hopefully your symptoms subside and we can get you home. How does that sound? OK, that sounds good. Thank you so much. Yeah, no problem. And I forgot, sorry we'll also probably do like a chest X Ray and a pulmonary function test. OK, sounds good. Yeah, no problem.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Presenting with worsening cough over the past month, constant coughing over the last week\n- Cough has been present for the last 5 years, diagnosed with COPD around the same time\n- Cough productive of white sputum, worse in the morning with sensation of \"gunk stuck in the back of throat\"\n- Sputum has had a yellowish tinge over the last week\n- Harsh cough, bringing up more sputum compared to before\n- Cough exacerbated by activity, associated with wheezing\n- Chest tightness during coughing fits, relieved with rest\n- Increased fatigue over the last few months\n- Bilateral ankle swelling for at least 1 year, tight shoes leaving sock marks\n\nPMHx:\n- COPD\n- HTN\n- Amoxicillin allergy (rash)\n\nMedications:\n- Lisinopril (HTN)\n- Ventolin prn\n- Spiriva\n\nSocHx:\n- Former smoker, quit a few years ago (1 ppd x30 years)\n- Occasional EtOH (1 beer/day)\n- Lives with wife in well-maintained condominium, works from home\n\nFHx:\n- Father had MI in 60s, alive\n- Paternal uncle had bladder cancer\n\nObjective:\n- Vitals pending\n\nAssessment:\n- COPD exacerbation\n- R/o COVID-19\n\nPlan:\n- COVID-19 swab, self-isolate x14 days, public health f/u if positive\n- Continue current COPD management (Ventolin prn, Spiriva)\n- Consider adding LABA +/- ICS if severe\n- Check O2 sat, target >88%, give O2 if needed\n- Azithromycin (amoxicillin allergy)\n- CXR\n- PFTs"}
{"ID": "soap_00072", "dialogue": "Just want to ask you few questions. Okay shoot. Do you have any nausea, vomiting? Yes to both. How about fever? Yes, I do. Any blood in sputum or chest pain? Yes, I do.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Symptoms: Nausea, vomiting, fever, blood in sputum, chest pain.\n- Severity: Not specified.\n- Duration: Not specified.\n- Associated Symptoms: None explicitly mentioned beyond those already listed.\n- Relevant Medical History: Not mentioned.\n- Family History: Not mentioned.\n- Allergies: Not mentioned.\n- Concerns: Patient is seeking clarification about their symptoms.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinician's interpretation or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education mentioned."}
{"ID": "soap_00027", "dialogue": "So, how much longer are you in juvie, young man? I've got about three weeks left, sir. Twenty five days, if you want to be exact. Good, time to make the best of getting out, right? Absolutely, I don't want to go back. Good. Do you smoke, young man? No sir, absolutely not.", "note": "Subjective:\n- Patient is currently in juvenile detention for approximately three weeks (25 days remaining).\n- Patient expresses a desire to make the best of the remaining time and does not want to return to juvenile detention.\n\nObjective:\n- No measurable or observable data provided.\n\nAssessment:\n- No clinical interpretations or diagnoses provided.\n\nPlan:\n- No actions, medications, tests, follow-up, or patient education explicitly mentioned."}
